<DocsLayout title="Lua Internals" description="How the OpenResty Lua code behaves at run time, collected from the modules' own comments" section="Architecture" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <p>This page collects what the comments in <code>ids/reverse_proxy_enhanced/lua/</code> say about how the
  modules behave at run time: where state is kept, which timers run, the tuned constants and the bugs behind
  them. The <a href="/docs/architecture#modules">module table</a> on the Architecture page lists the
  pure/adapter pairs; the routing stages are in <a href="/docs/architecture#pipeline">Request Pipeline</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="phases">Nginx Phases</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Phase</th><th>File</th><th>What runs</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>init_by_lua_file</code> (master, once)</td><td><code>init.lua</code></td><td>Builds <code>_G.config</code>, <code>_G.utils</code> and <code>_G.redis_pool</code>, seeds the shared dicts, and generates the session signing key before the workers fork, so every worker shares it.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>init_worker_by_lua_file</code> (each worker)</td><td><code>init_worker.lua</code></td><td>Seeds the random generator, loads the ready-pool list, warms backend connections after 3&nbsp;s, and — in worker 0 only — starts the <a href="#timers">background timers</a>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>access_by_lua_block</code> (each request)</td><td><code>nginx.conf</code></td><td>Maintenance gate, then honeytoken detection (a hit sets the score to 100), then <code>pattern_analyzer.analyze_request()</code> → <code>session_handler.get_session()</code> → <code>router.decide_route()</code> → <code>pool_router.get_or_assign_pool()</code> → <code>session_handler.update_session()</code>, followed by <code>sophistication_analyzer.classify_session()</code>. The result sets <code>$backend_upstream</code>.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2 id="globals">Globals and Shared Dicts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>_G.config</code> — every tunable parameter, mostly overridable from environment variables set in Compose. Passwords must come from the environment.</li>
    <li><code>_G.utils</code> — IP whitelist check (backed by <code>ip_rules.lua</code>, which handles IPv4, IPv6 and IPv4-mapped IPv6), UUIDs, risk-score sum, security-event logging.</li>
    <li><code>_G.redis_pool</code> — <code>get_connection()</code> / <code>close_connection()</code> over a per-worker keepalive pool to <code>session_store</code>, with one reconnect retry.</li>
  </ul>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Shared dict</th><th>Size</th><th>Holds</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>sessions</code></td><td>10&nbsp;MB</td><td>Worker-local cache of session JSON, saving Redis round-trips.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>threat_intel</code></td><td>10&nbsp;MB</td><td>Flagged IPs and user agents, backend health, the ready-pool list (<code>pool_list</code>), mirrored replication and maintenance flags, the WordPress install state.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>rate_limit</code></td><td>10&nbsp;MB</td><td>Request-rate counters for automation detection.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>honeypot_routes</code></td><td>1&nbsp;MB</td><td>Cached session → pool assignments.</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>The hot path never waits on Redis for flags: anything an external process sets in Redis (replication,
  maintenance, database-layer pool health) is copied into <code>threat_intel</code> by a timer, and requests read
  only the shared dict.</p>

  <h2 id="config">Configuration Notes</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Setting</th><th>Value</th><th>Why</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>redis.timeout</code> / <code>read_timeout</code></td><td>2000 / 5000&nbsp;ms</td><td>Raised from 1000 / 3000. Sporadic read timeouts were host scheduling stalls under load (empty Redis slowlog), not slow Redis; failing a whole background cycle over a 1&nbsp;s hiccup was worse than waiting.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>session.cookie_name</code></td><td><code>SERVERID</code></td><td>Looks like HAProxy's stickiness cookie. The old <code>HONEYPOT_SESSION</code> gave the deception away in the blind pentest. The <code>security</code> log format in <code>nginx.conf</code> hardcodes <code>$cookie_SERVERID</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>session.max_idle_time</code></td><td>3600&nbsp;s</td><td>Matches typical WooCommerce checkout session length.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>session.signing_key</code></td><td><code>SESSION_SIGNING_KEY</code></td><td>Without it a random key is made at start-up: old cookies stop verifying after a restart (sessions are recovered by fingerprint) and a bad MAC is not scored as tampering.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>session.recovery_window</code></td><td>900&nbsp;s</td><td>How long after a session's last request a cookie-less client with the same fingerprint is recovered into it (<code>SESSION_RECOVERY_WINDOW</code>).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>session.tamper_score</code></td><td>50</td><td>Added to a request whose cookie was edited or forged.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>gdpr.timestamp_window</code> / <code>binding_ttl</code></td><td>300 / 3600&nbsp;s</td><td>A replayed token stops matching once it ages out; a binding is prolonged on every match, so only an abandoned one expires.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>threat.honeypot_threshold</code></td><td>80</td><td>Raised from 50 to stop false positives on busy legitimate REST clients (WooCommerce mobile app, Stripe webhooks).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>threat.ip_reputation_cap</code></td><td>10</td><td>Address reputation alone can never divert a session; nothing at all is added once <code>shared_ip_sessions</code> (2) sessions used the address within <code>shared_ip_window</code> (3600&nbsp;s).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>threat.score_decay_half_life_seconds</code></td><td>300&nbsp;s</td><td>Added after one clean request was found to un-stick a session that had just matched a CVE. <code>THREAT_DECAY_ENABLED=false</code> sets it to 0, which disables decay.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>decay_offense_slowdown</code> / <code>permaflag_offenses</code></td><td>0.75 / 5</td><td>Each recorded offense multiplies the half-life by 1.75; at 5 offenses, or a peak at <code>permaflag_score</code>, decay stops (<code>decay_policy.lua</code>).</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>The operator whitelist (loopback, the Tailscale ranges and <code>10.0.0.0/8</code>) gives a −20 reputation and
  is never diverted.</p>

  <h2 id="timers">Background Timers</h2>
  <p>All periodic tasks run in worker 0 only. The health-check timer once ran in every worker; their checks
  fired at the same instant, so one moment of contention counted as several consecutive failures and marked a
  healthy backend UNHEALTHY. Moving it under the worker-0 guard fixed both the extra load and the false
  positive.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Every</th><th>Task</th></tr>
    <tr><td>5&nbsp;s</td><td>Refresh the ready-pool list from <code>honeypot_pool:ready</code> into the shared dict (first run after 1&nbsp;s).</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5&nbsp;s</td><td>Mirror replication, maintenance and (database layer) pool-health flags from Redis into <code>threat_intel</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>15&nbsp;s</td><td>Health-check <code>production_backend</code> and every ready pool.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>30&nbsp;s</td><td>Probe <code>/wp-admin/install.php</code> on production for the install state.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>30&nbsp;s</td><td>Read new Suricata <code>eve.json</code> alerts into <code>threat_ips</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>60&nbsp;s</td><td>Clean up rate-limit counters.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>300&nbsp;s</td><td>Evict expired sessions from the local dict (<code>session.cleanup_interval</code>).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>blacklist_refresh_hours</code></td><td>AbuseIPDB blacklist pull (first run after 15&nbsp;s), only when <code>ABUSEIPDB_BLACKLIST_ENABLED</code>. The pull is cached in Redis, so restarts don't spend the daily quota.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2 id="redis">Redis Key Schema</h2>
  <p><code>pool_router_rules.lua</code> defines the keys <code>pool_manager</code> and the router share; the rest belong to
  the session and threat modules.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Key</th><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>honeypot_pool:ready</code></td><td>SET</td><td>Pool numbers provisioned and healthy.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>honeypot_pool:free</code></td><td>ZSET</td><td>Ready pools with no owner (score = pool number).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>honeypot_pool:owner:&lt;N&gt;</code></td><td>SET</td><td>Sessions assigned to pool N.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>honeypot_pool:counter</code></td><td>INT</td><td>Round-robin counter for borrowing and sharing.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>honeypot_pool:provision</code></td><td>LIST</td><td>"A session was just assigned" wake-ups for <code>pool_manager</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>honeypot_pool:capped</code></td><td>STR (TTL)</td><td>Set by <code>pool_manager</code> while no further pool may start (budget, <code>POOL_MAX</code>, host load).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>honeypot_pool:waiting</code></td><td>ZSET</td><td>Sessions borrowing a pool until theirs is built (score = when they started waiting); at most 200, oldest dropped.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>honeypot_pool:waiting_seen</code></td><td>HASH</td><td>Last request time of each waiting session; quiet ones are skipped at hand-off.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>honeypot_pool:last_seen</code></td><td>HASH</td><td>Last time a request was routed to each pool, written at most every 10&nbsp;s; drives the idle scale-down.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>honeypot_pool:health</code></td><td>HASH</td><td>Database layer only: each pool database's container state, published by <code>pool_manager</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>honeypot_pool_session:&lt;SID&gt;</code></td><td>STR (24&nbsp;h)</td><td>A session's pool assignment.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>honeypot_pool_replicating:&lt;N&gt;</code></td><td>STR</td><td>Set by the content-replication script while pool N is being synced.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>honeypot_maintenance</code></td><td>STR (TTL)</td><td>Set by the dashboard during a WordPress update.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>fp_session:&lt;fingerprint&gt;</code></td><td>STR</td><td>Passive fingerprint → session, for recovery after cookies are cleared.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>session_cookie_rejections</code></td><td>LIST</td><td>The last 1000 rejected (edited or forged) cookies.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>threat_ips</code></td><td>STR (JSON)</td><td>Per-IP reputation; read-modify-write through <code>threat_intel.lua</code>, which also mirrors it into the shared dict.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>honeytoken:&lt;id&gt;</code></td><td>STR (30&nbsp;days)</td><td>Injected honeytokens and their use counts.</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>Pools 1–3 are declared in Compose with an nginx <code>upstream honeypot_backend_N</code> block; higher numbers
  (up to a sanity ceiling of 64) are created by <code>pool_manager</code> and reached by container DNS name. The
  names <code>honeypot_eshop_N</code>, <code>honeypot_database_N</code> and <code>honeypot_backend_N</code> must match
  Compose exactly.</p>

  <p>To inspect these keys on a running stack, pass the password from <code>.env</code> (the
  <code>session_store</code> container does not have it in its environment):</p>
  <pre><code>cd ids
REDIS_PW="$(grep ^REDIS_PASSWORD= .env | cut -d= -f2-)"
rcli() &#123; docker compose exec -T session_store redis-cli -a "$REDIS_PW" --no-auth-warning "$@"; &#125;

rcli SMEMBERS honeypot_pool:ready              # healthy pools
rcli ZRANGE honeypot_pool:free 0 -1            # unowned spares
rcli ZRANGE honeypot_pool:waiting 0 -1 WITHSCORES
rcli GET honeypot_pool_session:&lt;SID&gt;           # one session's pool
rcli GET threat_ips                            # per-IP reputation (JSON)</code></pre>

  <h2 id="assign">Pool Assignment</h2>
  <p><code>ASSIGN_SCRIPT</code> runs inside Redis with <code>EVAL</code>, so two nginx workers can never hand one free pool
  to two sessions. It returns a pool number and a mode:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>existing / pending</strong> — the session already has a pool; its TTL is refreshed.</li>
    <li><strong>exclusive</strong> — a free ready pool becomes the session's own, and a wake-up asks
    <code>pool_manager</code> to build the next spare.</li>
    <li><strong>pending</strong> — no free pool but growth is allowed: borrow a ready pool round-robin and join
    the waiting queue (case A).</li>
    <li><strong>shared</strong> — growth is capped: share a ready pool round-robin for good (case B).</li>
    <li><strong>none</strong> — no ready pool at all.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The free-pool and fallback part of the script (<code>pool_router_rules.lua</code>); <code>KEYS[2]</code> is
  the free set, <code>KEYS[3]</code> the ready set, <code>KEYS[4]</code> the counter, <code>KEYS[6]</code> the cap
  flag and <code>KEYS[7]</code> the waiting queue:</p>
  <pre><code>while true do
  local popped = redis.call('ZPOPMIN', KEYS[2])
  if not popped or #popped == 0 then break end
  if redis.call('SISMEMBER', KEYS[3], popped[1]) == 1 then
    pool = tonumber(popped[1])
    mode = 'exclusive'
    break
  end
end
if not pool then
  local ready = redis.call('SMEMBERS', KEYS[3])
  if #ready == 0 then
    return &#123;0, 'none'&#125;
  end
  local nums = &#123;&#125;
  for i, v in ipairs(ready) do nums[i] = tonumber(v) end
  table.sort(nums)
  local c = redis.call('INCR', KEYS[4])
  pool = nums[((c - 1) % #nums) + 1]
  if redis.call('EXISTS', KEYS[6]) == 1 then
    mode = 'shared'
  else
    mode = 'pending'
    redis.call('ZADD', KEYS[7], tonumber(ARGV[4]) or c, ARGV[1])
    -- ... trimmed to max_waiting (200), oldest dropped
  end
end</code></pre>

  <p>Assignments are cached in <code>honeypot_routes</code> for 300&nbsp;s, but a pending one for only 3&nbsp;s, so the
  move to a session's own pool takes effect quickly. A cached pool that is no longer in the ready list is a
  cache miss (this fixed the 502s after scale-down). If the assigned pool is unhealthy or being replicated,
  the request goes to the next healthy pool without changing the stored assignment; if none is healthy it
  goes to the assigned pool and nginx returns the error. Missing or malformed health data counts as healthy.</p>

  <h2 id="health">Health Checks</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Probe <code>/robots.txt</code> every 15&nbsp;s with an 8&nbsp;s timeout; 5 failures mark a backend unhealthy, 2
    successes healthy again. Previously 10&nbsp;s / 5&nbsp;s / 3: a post-start load burst flipped all three pools
    unhealthy on slow renders while Docker still reported them healthy. A truly dead backend is still caught within
    about 75&nbsp;s.</li>
    <li>The timeout constant used to be declared but ignored (a hardcoded 2000&nbsp;ms), so tuning it did nothing; a
    heavy page with 30+ concurrent assets once tripped it.</li>
    <li>Every internal probe sends the User-Agent <code>honeypot-internal-healthprobe</code>, which Suricata rules
    sid:1000056 and sid:1000060 exclude so the probes don't raise "High Rate HTTP Requests" or "Rapid
    Vulnerability Scanning". The string is mirrored in <code>ids/suricata_rules/local.rules</code>; change both. It
    is deliberately not the public <code>resty.http</code> default, which an adversary could spoof.</li>
    <li>Production's health is for monitoring only; nginx's own <code>max_fails</code> / <code>fail_timeout</code>
    handle it. Log lines name the containers behind an upstream, not just the upstream alias.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="suricata">Suricata Ingestion</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Reads <code>eve.json</code>, not <code>fast.log</code>: the old regex had 8 captures into 7 variables, so
    <code>src_ip</code> received the classification text and alerts never reached the real IP.</li>
    <li>Resume position: a Redis error skips the cycle (instead of rescanning a 600&nbsp;MB+ file inside worker 0's
    event loop); a missing key starts at the end of the file; a position past the end (truncated file) restarts at
    0. One cycle reads at most 8&nbsp;MB, and a half-written last line is left for the next cycle.</li>
    <li>A cheap substring check skips the many flow/netflow events before any JSON decode.</li>
    <li>Score per alert by Suricata severity: 1 → 50, 2 → 25, otherwise 10, capped at 100. The stored score only
    rises; decay is applied when it is read. The reason carries the matched signature through to the routing
    reason.</li>
    <li>Private and loopback <code>src_ip</code> values are container-to-container hops and are not written to
    <code>threat_ips</code>; the raw alert is still kept (last 1000).</li>
    <li>A missing or unreadable <code>eve.json</code> is logged at most once per 10 minutes rather than looking like
    "no alerts".</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="identity">Session Identity</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Session ids are 16 bytes from OpenSSL's CSPRNG (previously a guessable SHA-1 of time, <code>math.random</code>
    and the worker PID). The cookie is <code>&lt;id&gt;.&lt;HMAC&gt;</code>, compared in constant time; a cookie with a
    bad MAC is never looked up.</li>
    <li>A cookie-less request is matched through <code>fp_session:&lt;fingerprint&gt;</code>: the TLS ClientHello
    (GREASE stripped), HTTP version, User-Agent, Accept-Language/-Encoding, the low-entropy client hints and, by
    default, the IP. The pointer is refreshed on every session update and lives <code>recovery_window</code> after
    the last request, so a neighbour behind the same NAT with an identical browser is merged only while the
    session is active.</li>
    <li>The GDPR signature runs alongside, never instead. It fails open: a missing key, a forged token or a Redis
    error just means no GDPR signal for that request. See <a href="/docs/detection/signature">GDPR Signature</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="gates">Maintenance and Install Gates</h2>
  <p><strong>Maintenance</strong> (<code>maintenance.lua</code>): during a WordPress update the dashboard sets
  <code>honeypot_maintenance</code> with a TTL, the mirror timer copies it, and the first thing in the access phase
  serves a 503 page with <code>Retry-After: 120</code> to production and honeypot alike. Only an explicit
  <code>"1"</code> counts as on, and the TTL brings the site back on its own if the dashboard dies mid-update. See
  <a href="/docs/engine/wordpress-management">WordPress Management</a>.</p>
  <p>The gate at the top of the access phase in <code>nginx.conf</code>:</p>
  <pre><code>local threat_intel = ngx.shared.threat_intel
local maintenance = require "maintenance"
if threat_intel and maintenance.should_serve_maintenance(threat_intel:get("maintenance")) then
    ngx.status = 503
    ngx.header["Content-Type"] = "text/html; charset=utf-8"
    ngx.header["Retry-After"] = maintenance.RETRY_AFTER
    ngx.header["Cache-Control"] = "no-store"
    ngx.say(maintenance.page_html(maintenance.RETRY_AFTER))
    return ngx.exit(503)
end</code></pre>

  <p><strong>Install state</strong> (<code>wp_install_state.lua</code>): <code>install.php</code> is normally scored
  hard, but it is legitimate before the first-run wizard. Nginx has no MySQL driver, so the probe looks for
  WordPress's own "already installed" text. Only production is probed, since pool databases are copies of it. Any
  probe error keeps the previous value, and unknown or stale state counts as installed (strict), so an outage can
  never open the fast path.</p>

  <h2 id="honeytokens">Honeytokens</h2>
  <p><code>honeytoken_handler.lua</code> plants fake high-value artefacts in honeypot responses and treats their later
  use as confirmed compromise:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Token</th><th>Planted in</th><th>Detected when</th></tr>
    <tr><td>HT-APIKEY</td><td>WooCommerce REST <code>/wp-json/wc/v3/</code> responses</td><td>Used in an Authorization header</td></tr>
    <tr><td>HT-DBCREDS</td><td>A "leaked" <code>wp-config.php</code> on config probes (non-existent internal DB host)</td><td>Host, user or password appear in a POST body</td></tr>
    <tr><td>HT-ADMINPW</td><td>An admin-note page</td><td>The hash or plaintext appears in a login POST</td></tr>
    <tr><td>HT-S3URL</td><td>Media library responses</td><td>The URL or its access key id is requested</td></tr>
    <tr><td>HT-JWTKEY</td><td>Plugin-settings REST response</td><td>JWTs signed with it appear as Bearer tokens</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>Values are SHA-1 derivations of a per-deployment salt, so they are the same across workers and restarts and
  need no per-variant storage. Detection runs before routing; a hit sets the request's score to 100, logs to ELK and Redis,
  and records time-to-use from the injection timestamp.</p>

  <p>In the access phase, a honeytoken hit overrides whatever the pattern analyser scored:</p>
  <pre><code>local ht_result = honeytoken_handler.analyze_request_for_tokens()
-- ...
local pattern_result = pattern_analyzer.analyze_request(ngx.var.request_uri, request_headers, ngx.var.remote_addr)
if ht_result then
    pattern_result.score = 100
    pattern_result.suspicious = true
    table.insert(pattern_result.patterns_matched, "honeytoken_reuse:" .. ht_result.token_id)
end</code></pre>

  <h2 id="classifiers">Content Classifiers</h2>
  <p><strong>Prompt-injection filter</strong>: not wired to any LLM yet (the LLM response feature is planned), but
  <code>pattern_analyzer</code> runs <code>detect()</code> on every request because injection text is anomalous on a
  storefront anyway; half its risk score is added. Patterns cover five categories — instruction override, role
  hijack, prompt exfiltration, context break (code fences, punctuation floods) and base64-encoded payloads.
  <code>sanitize()</code> exists for the future LLM path.</p>
  <p><strong>Sophistication analyzer</strong>: a rule-based, auditable classifier (scripted / AI-assisted / manual /
  unknown) from inter-request timing regularity (gaps over 5 minutes ignored; sub-second pacing is scripted),
  header completeness, User-Agent category and payload precision. It runs after routing, does no I/O, and returns
  every contributing signal so the label can be checked in the logs.</p>

  <h2 id="dblayer">Database-Layer Variant</h2>
  <p><code>ids/db_proxy/reverse_proxy_enhanced/lua/</code> overrides only <code>init_worker.lua</code> and
  <code>health_check.lua</code>. With one <code>production_eshop</code> choosing the database per request (via
  <code>wp-content/db.php</code>), there are no <code>honeypot_eshop_N</code> backends to warm, health-check or mirror
  replication flags for — probing them is what used to log endless "honeypot_backend_N marked as UNHEALTHY".
  Instead the worker copies <code>honeypot_pool:health</code> into the health cache, so a session whose database is
  down is steered to another until it returns. See <a href="/docs/engine/layers">Honeypot Layers</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="conventions">Conventions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pure/adapter split.</strong> Decisions live in <code>*_rules.lua</code> with no <code>ngx.*</code> or
    <code>_G.*</code>; adapters do the I/O. <code>router.decide_route()</code> stays in the adapter on purpose: its
    stages interleave session writes with Redis I/O, and it is the most safety-critical function in the codebase.</li>
    <li><strong>One implementation per helper.</strong> <code>url_decode</code> and <code>escape_pattern</code> moved to
    <code>lua_pattern_utils.lua</code> once there were three copies; the two <code>is_static_asset</code> copies disagreed
    on query strings (<code>style.css?v=123</code>) and the router now uses the stripping one.</li>
    <li><strong><code>ngx.null</code> is not <code>nil</code>.</strong> A missing Redis key returns the
    <code>ngx.null</code> sentinel, which once crashed <code>cjson.decode</code> on a fresh Redis;
    <code>threat_intel.load()</code> handles it once for every caller.</li>
    <li><strong>Fail safe, in the right direction.</strong> Maintenance, GDPR and health data fail open (site
    stays up); the install-state gate fails strict. Optional modules are loaded with <code>pcall(require, …)</code>
    and disable themselves with a log line.</li>
    <li><strong>No dead code.</strong> A module ships only with a live call path — hence the prompt-injection
    filter feeding scores today, and upload analysis folded into the session score instead of its own location,
    where detections never outlived the request.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Because the rules modules are pure, a test is a plain Lua file with no OpenResty. Each suite sets
  <code>package.path = "../?.lua;"</code>, so it runs from the <code>tests/</code> directory:</p>
  <pre><code>-- tests/test_decay_policy.lua
check("1 offense @0.75 -&gt; 1.75x", approx(dp.half_life_multiplier(1, 0.75), 1.75))
check("one half-life halves it (no offenses)", approx(dp.decay(100, HL, HL, 0, &#123;&#125;), 50))
check("maxed score never decays (permaflag)", dp.decay(100, 10 * HL, HL, 1, cfg) == 100)</code></pre>
  <pre><code># every suite (exits non-zero if any fails)
ids/reverse_proxy_enhanced/lua/tests/run_all.sh

# one suite
cd ids/reverse_proxy_enhanced/lua/tests &amp;&amp; lua test_decay_policy.lua</code></pre>
